ICML-2025-P0022,ICML-2025-A000110,Harshvardhan Agarwal,stacked,2,2,2,"as a prompt x1 , y1 , . . . , xk , yk , x∗ , they predict a ŷ indicative of having learned the task. The success of ICL has been demonstrated on several tasks (Bertsch et al., ; Agarwal et al., ), including novel key-value mappings (Kossen et al., ), and new synthetic language learning tasks (Rajaraman et al., ; Bietti et al., ; Edelman et al., ; Akyürek et al., ). For such unseen tasks, a widely accepted explanation is that ICL arises by the formation of induction circuits (Olsson et al., ; Reddy, ; Singh et al., ). An induction circuit requires at least two layers for formation as illustrated in Figure . In the first stage, each label position i copies over its previous xi as a key. In the second stage, the test input x∗ is used as a query to copy over the label yi (i ≤ k) with the matching key xi . This explanation also works for learning new languages where the key corresponds to n-grams (Rajaraman et al., ; Bietti et al., ; Edelman et al., ; Akyürek et al., ).",-2,data/raw/icml/2025/first_pages/ICML-2025-P0022.txt,https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v267/main/assets/agarwal25d/agarwal25d.pdf
ICML-2025-P0022,ICML-2025-A000111,Sunita Sarawagi,stacked,2,2,2,"as a prompt x1 , y1 , . . . , xk , yk , x∗ , they predict a ŷ indicative of having learned the task. The success of ICL has been demonstrated on several tasks (Bertsch et al., ; Agarwal et al., ), including novel key-value mappings (Kossen et al., ), and new synthetic language learning tasks (Rajaraman et al., ; Bietti et al., ; Edelman et al., ; Akyürek et al., ). For such unseen tasks, a widely accepted explanation is that ICL arises by the formation of induction circuits (Olsson et al., ; Reddy, ; Singh et al., ). An induction circuit requires at least two layers for formation as illustrated in Figure . In the first stage, each label position i copies over its previous xi as a key. In the second stage, the test input x∗ is used as a query to copy over the label yi (i ≤ k) with the matching key xi . This explanation also works for learning new languages where the key corresponds to n-grams (Rajaraman et al., ; Bietti et al., ; Edelman et al., ; Akyürek et al., ).",-6,data/raw/icml/2025/first_pages/ICML-2025-P0022.txt,https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v267/main/assets/agarwal25d/agarwal25d.pdf
