ICML-2025-P2780,ICML-2025-A014438,Yi Xu,stacked,2,4,4,"gpt- - claude- -sonnet- Yi-34B-Chat Qwen1. -72B-Chat Llama- -70B-Instruct claude- -opus- gpt-4o- - - gpt- - -preview > gpt-4o- - - gpt- -turbo- - - gpt-4o- - - > gpt- - -preview gpt- - -preview Llama- -70B-Instruct as the baseline: claude- -opus- as the baseline: yi-large-preview-verified Automatic evaluation methods based on large language models (LLMs) are emerging as the standard tool for assessing the instruction-following abilities of LLM-based agents. The most common method in this paradigm, pairwise comparisons with a baseline model, critically depends on the assumption of transitive preferences. However, the validity of this assumption remains largely unexplored. In this study, we investigate the presence of non-transitivity within the AlpacaEval framework and analyze its effects on model rankings. We find that LLM judges exhibit non-transitive preferences, leading to rankings that are sensitive to the choice of the baseline model. To mitigate this issue, we show that round-robin tournaments combined with Bradley-Terry models of preference can produce more reliable rankings. Notably, our method increases both the Spearman correlation and the Kendall correlation with Chatbot Arena ( . % → . % and . % → . % respectively). To address the computational cost of round-robin tournaments, we propose Swiss-Wise Iterative Matchmaking (S WIM) tournaments, using a dynamic matching strategy to capture the benefits of round-robin tournaments while maintaining computational efficiency. Llama- . -405B-Instruct-Turbo",-4,data/raw/icml/2025/first_pages/ICML-2025-P2780.txt,https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v267/main/assets/xu25w/xu25w.pdf
